          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"category\":\"detector_wide_pulse_feature_shift\",\"cause\":\"Unknown; the leading hypothesis is a run-wide digitizer, pulse-finding, or AutoDQM reference/configuration mismatch rather than a localized PMT or LVDS failure.\",\"action\":\"First verify the raw per-channel pulse counts, waveform baselines, pulse heights/areas, digitizer configuration, and pulse-finding settings against a known-good run. Check whether the million-scale z-scores result from zero or near-zero reference dispersion. Also inspect board matching, synchronization, queue occupancy, and digitizer-versus-trigger-board rates. If raw waveforms are normal, correct the AutoDQM reference or feature normalization; if digitizer data are globally malformed or unmatched, restart/resynchronize MilliDAQ and the affected digitizers only after confirming that diagnosis.\",\"confidence\":0.58,\"supporting_historical_runs\":[1605,1642,2126],\"reasoning_summary\":\"OBSERVED: all 129 subruns are anomalous, with 10,816 channel-entries and widespread pulse-count, height, area, and duration features; channels 47, 50, 69, 57, and 25 have million-scale z-scores. Trigger rate remains stable near 4.86 Hz, and LVDS has no persistent zero, missing, low, or high signal pins. The final count drop occurs without a comparable rate collapse and may reflect a shorter final subrun. INFERRED: the detector-wide persistence and normal trigger/LVDS summaries favor a global data-processing, digitizer-state, or reference-normalization problem over one broken PMT base or LVDS connection. Runs 1605, 1642, and 2126 share some million-scale nPulses anomalies or channels, but none is an exact match; their diagnosed CPU, PMT, and LVDS failures require telemetry or localized missing-channel evidence absent here. UNKNOWN: raw feature values, reference dispersions, board matching, queue state, synchronization, digitizer rates, and within-run configuration changes. These checks are needed to distinguish a scoring artifact from genuine DAQ-wide waveform corruption.\"}"
        }
      ],
